{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RCA (Phase 1 complete — confirmed root cause; escalating the fix as a design decision, see OPTIONS).\n\nMECHANISM (confirmed): src/rebar/_store/event_commit_git.py binds every lock-held git child of the event-append write path with _GIT_TIMEOUT = 30 (line 61). gitutil.run_git_bounded folds a TimeoutExpired into a synthetic rc-124 result with stderr \"git timed out after 30s\" — and that marker is classified TRANSPORT-retriable ONLY (git_outcome.py:142-144, TRANSPORT_RETRIABLE_MARKERS). Verified at runtime: git_outcome.is_transient_fs(\"git timed out after 30s\") == False, is_index_lock == False. So neither retry wrapper on the commit path (_with_index_lock_retry outer, _with_transient_fault_retry inner) rides it out: ONE slow-but-healthy `git commit` exceeding 30s is killed and the write fails terminally with \"Error: git commit failed while holding lock: git timed out after 30s\" (event_append.py:196-216). Under full-suite xdist load the host degrades local commit latency enough to cross 30s: unloaded baseline in a fresh temp store on this host is 47-404 ms/commit (30s = ~75-600x degradation), and the failing F07 node alone took 55.48 s wall, showing the suite-time host was orders of magnitude slower.\n\nDISCRIMINATING EXPERIMENT (repeatable, satisfies AC-1): a PATH git shim that sleeps 3 s only on `commit` (healthy git otherwise), driven through rebar.create_ticket in an isolated temp store, two arms differing ONLY in the bound:\n- ARM A _GIT_TIMEOUT=2: FAILED \"rebar create failed (exit 1): Error: git commit failed while holding lock: git timed out after 2s\", wall 2.5 s, exactly ONE commit child launched (no retry).\n- ARM B _GIT_TIMEOUT=120 (gitutil._LOCAL_GIT_TIMEOUT): SUCCESS, wall 4.3 s, one commit child.\nSame store, same git, same \"load\" — the bound-vs-duration relation alone decides the outcome. This rejects the rivals: NOT index.lock contention (isolated per-test temp store, no peer; error text would name index.lock and the lock retry would fire), NOT store-lock acquire loss (royal-weariless-zebrafish / 10c3-5a33 is a LockTimeout BEFORE the write body; here the mkdir lock was already held), NOT a fixture defect (the identical fixture succeeds in ARM B).\n\nWHY 30s IS THE WRONG CALIBRATION (defect, not environment): the 30 was copied from the NETWORK push path — c2ba chose it explicitly to match _store/push.py and tests/unit/test_store_write_git_timeout_c2ba.py:66 pins event_append._GIT_TIMEOUT == _push._GIT_TIMEOUT. The later 9305 work (icicled-unstable-urial, operator-ratified 2026-08-04: local git contention is absorbed AUTOMATICALLY, only a genuinely stuck lock surfaces an error) calibrated the sibling seam for the SAME class of ops at watchdog grade: gitutil._LOCAL_GIT_TIMEOUT = 120, with the in-repo rationale \"deliberately NOT copied from the 30s/300s network values — these are small local add/commits ... 120s distinguishes 'wedged fs/lock' from 'slow' with ~100x margin\", and left event_append at its own 30s. A latency-grade network bound on a local lock-held commit violates the ratified absorb-don't-escalate invariant the moment the host is merely slow rather than wedged.\n\nRELATIONSHIP TO PRIOR TICKETS (AC-5):\n- moldavite-evasive-elephant (9590-34f5): its live run 31362329118 (2026-08-10) contains the FIRST recorded occurrence of this exact failure — test_outbound_create_stamps_both_provenance_markers failed with the identical \"git commit failed while holding lock: git timed out after 30s\" — classified there as runner I/O contention with the direction \"if it recurs it needs its own ticket\". It recurred (F07 suite run, this ticket); a comment there mis-filed it under royal-weariless-zebrafish, which is a different mechanism (lock ACQUIRE loss, not an in-lock git kill). Two occurrences on two different hosts/tests = a failure class, not a one-off environmental fault.\n- royal-weariless-zebrafish (10c3-5a33): pre-lock acquire budget; explicitly does NOT cover a timeout after acquisition — confirmed distinct by the experiment above.\n- 3d57-602f (show-stall scan collapse, merged 14aa285f963): READ-path scan cost; different mechanism, does not explain or fix this. It corroborates only that heavy concurrent load is rebar's normal operating mode.\n\nDEFECT ORIGIN (caused_by): commit 3aec0ae90ab \"c2ba: bound review-bot shutdown & store-write git\" (rebar-ticket: c2ba-98bf-ca2d-481a) introduced _GIT_TIMEOUT=30 on this path; before it the lock-held git children were UNBOUNDED, so a 55 s commit would have succeeded — the date test places the wrong-behavior transition exactly at that commit. (The constant moved file in 641ba8bed16, a carrier split, walked through.) 9305/42076d367e9 is a contributing miss — its research proved 30 s is a network-grade value and recalibrated the sibling seam to 120 s but left this constant — yet the introducing change is c2ba. caused_by link recorded bug->c2ba.\n\nWHY NOT AUTO-FIXED (design decision, operator escalation): every remedy changes a ratified timeout model:\n- OPTION A (recommended): adopt the 9305 watchdog calibration — event_commit_git._GIT_TIMEOUT = gitutil._LOCAL_GIT_TIMEOUT (120 s). Consequences to rule on: (1) breaks the c2ba parity contract test (test_store_write_git_timeout_c2ba.py:66 asserting ==_push._GIT_TIMEOUT — that test would need to assert the 9305 value instead); (2) lengthens the worst-case lock-held store write, an input to the review-bot stop_grace_period computability argument c2ba was opened for (150 s grace vs up to ~120 s per stuck git child, and a write issues several children).\n- OPTION B: classify the rc-124 fold as retriable on the local write path (add \"git timed out after\" to the transient set, or retry rc-124 in _with_transient_fault_retry). Bounded (attempts x 30 s) but does NOT fix the class: a commit needing 40 s is killed on every attempt; also repeatedly SIGKILLing a mid-commit git churns .git/index.lock/loose objects. Not recommended alone.\n- OPTION C: treat as environment (suite parallelism/runner sizing). Rejected by the evidence: rebar's stated normal operating mode is many concurrent local agents (9305 context), and the invariant says slow-but-healthy must be absorbed.\nRegression coverage (AC-2/3) should land WITH the chosen option: the ARM A/B shim experiment converts directly into a RED test that a slow-but-healthy commit under the chosen policy does not terminally lose the write."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787443858022963001,"uuid":"a3764ff5-4cc9-41e0-9039-dcd19732c7a4"}